Fillets: a rounding whose contact on a narrow face N of its edge runs off
N across N's tangent edge with a face C and comes back keeps its ball on
N's surface run on past that edge, and C cuts the rounding where it runs
into C (mitcad#343).

The case: a closed chain of 36 edges round a part, rounded with 5. Along
three of its edges the face N on one side is a cylinder band of radius 55
about 2.47 wide, between the edge and a torus C of minor radius 10 tangent
to it; the rounding's contact on N lies 2.59 from the edge (the faces meet
at 54.7 degrees), so the contact leaves N across the tangent edge and
comes back near both ends of the band, where N widens. The walk took C
for N between the crossings: the ball rolled on over C, which curves away
from N's surface, so it sat up to 0.18 deeper than on N's surface, and the
rounding removed 7.1 mm3 more at each of the three places (-246.50 mm3 in
all against the stored body's -225.30; now -226.48). The bodies stored in
the files keep the ball on N's surface run on (its centre 5 from N's
cylinder all along), and C cuts the rounding along their intersection, a
crease of up to 0.19 rad, the rest of N there left as strips 1.6 long at
the crossings.

- ChFi3d_Builder::KeepFacesAcrossTangentEdges (in Compute after
  JoinAcrossCorners, every stripe of constant radius): a run of a stripe's
  pieces on N and Fo, on C (N's tangent neighbour across the edge the
  contact crossed) and on N and Fo again, the ball rolling on C between
  (the blend tangent to C there; pieces already cut by C at a crease, as
  patch 0160 cuts a blend walked over a rounded hole's mouth, are left as
  they are), is walked again from the first
  piece's start to the last piece's end, N's surface unbounded
  (KeepFaceAcrossTangentEdge), along a copy of the stripe's elementary
  spine, or, where analytic pieces at the run's ends have none, along the
  spine itself interpolated over the run. Where the walk reaches both ends
  at the pieces' sections, its contact on N leaves N once and comes back,
  and the contact lies off C's surface by a tenth of the radius at most (a
  slight overflow, as in the stored designs; deeper ones are left as they
  were), the run becomes three pieces on the walked surface, one face: up
  to where the contact leaves N, the cut by C (its side on C), and from
  where the contact comes back on. The spine's edges generate that face
  (Generated) in place of the pieces' faces.
- ChFi3d_SectionCut: the cut, section by section, from the contact on N
  towards the other one, the first point on C's surface. C meets the blend
  tangentially where the contact crosses the tangent edge, where an
  intersection of the two surfaces leaves gaps; the cut there is the
  contact itself. Its curve and p-curves are interpolated by chord length
  (it leaves the contact like a square root of the spine parameter).
